{{also|bulle|bullé}}
==German==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈbʊlə/|lang=de}}

===Etymology 1===
Via {{etym|nds-de|de}} from {{etym|gml|de}} {{term|bulle|lang=gml}}. Cognate with {{etym|nl|-}} {{term|bul|lang=nl}}. More at [[bull]].

====Noun====
{{de-noun|m|Bullen|Bullen}}

# [[bull]] (male cattle)
# {{context|figurative|lang=de}} [[bull]] {{gloss|strong or stout man}}

=====Usage notes=====
* Though generally interchangeable with {{term|Stier|lang=de}}, only ''Bulle'' is commonly used in agricultural contexts in northern and central Germany. The word is less frequent in the South.

=====Declension=====
{{de-decl-noun-m|n|n}}

=====Synonyms=====
* {{l|de|Stier}}

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|de|bullig}}

===Etymology 2===
Documented since the 19th century. Probably from {{term|Polizist|lang=de}} (or an abbreviation thereof), whose first syllable may in some dialects have become homophonous to [[#Etymology_1|Bulle]]. — Another possibility is a derivation from {{term|Puller|lang=de}} or {{term|Landpuller|lang=de}}, a non-derogatory term for a policeman of the 18th century.

====Noun====
{{de-noun|m|Bullen|Bullen}}

# {{context|mildly|pejorative|lang=de}} [[police officer]], [[cop]], [[pig]]

=====Usage notes=====
* The word is only mildly pejorative in colloquial speech, it is nevertheless punishable in Germany to use it towards or in reference to a police officer on duty.

=====Declension=====
{{de-decl-noun-m|n|n}}

=====Related terms=====
* [[Bullette]] {{qualifier|female form; humorous}}

===Etymology 3===
From {{etyl|la|de}} ''[[bulla]]'' "seal capsule", originally meaning "ball, bullet".

====Noun====
{{de-noun|f||Bullen}}

# [[bull|Bull]] (papal decree).

=====Declension=====
{{de-decl-noun-f|n}}

